{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1532251-necropolii","id":1532251,"title":"Necropolii","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Wraith: The Oblivion"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-03-08T13:15:01-08:00","id":1532251,"title":"Necropolii","content":"Might I please ask if there is any list of each Necropolis<br /> mentioned in official sources?<br /> <br /> I know that not every city has it\u2019s own Necropolis and I\u2019m interested in finding out whether there\u2019s a specific type of city that is required to support such an outpost of Stygia.<br /> <br /> Incidentally, it has also occurred to me to wonder how long it took for the living city to which a Necropolis was attached to equal the population of it\u2019s sepulchral counterpart (Given that for much of human history people lived fast and died young).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw","name":"Atticus Cleanclaw","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"638"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-03-09T05:52:32-08:00","id":1532286,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Atticus Cleanclaw</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1532251-necropolii#post1532251\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Might I please ask if there is any list of each Necropolis mentioned in official sources?</div> </div> </div> </div>Not that I know of. I think the general assumption is that if a place has enough Wraiths within the Dark Kingdom of Iron it qualifies for a Citadel, so an exhaustive list would be a bit pointless. It's a bit like asking whether a city qualifies as a metropolis or not. The relative size and strategic importance within the region seems to be a factor, too. A comparatively smaller city might become a Necropolis if it is the largest settlement within a region.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Atticus Cleanclaw</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1532251-necropolii#post1532251\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I know that not every city has it\u2019s own Necropolis and I\u2019m interested in finding out whether there\u2019s a specific type of city that is required to support such an outpost of Stygia.</div> </div> </div> </div>As mentioned, for large cities a Citadel seems to be the default assumption. I can think of a few special cases that influence whether or not a city becomes a Necropolis:<br /> <br /> - Large Renegade or Heretic enclaves<br /> Probably rare and not all that large. According to the books they dot the landscape between greater Necropoles or exist inside them in hiding. There are some spots in which a city is contested (Dublin, Paris) but as far as I know, there aren't any large cities within the Dark Kingdom of Iron that are under Renegade or Heretic control proper. Perhaps with the exception of Vatican City.<br /> <br /> - Places of large calamities or violence<br /> The Dark Kingdom of Wire being the prime example here, but similar places could exist on a smaller scale elsewhere. Something caused the death of a large number of people and the Wraiths stuck to the place, resulting in a large Shadowland settlement without a Citadel. Although many of these would have been reclaimed by Stygia in time, some may have kept their independence. I see those mostly situated in border regions with other Dark Kingdoms.<br /> <br /> - Fallen Citadels<br /> Again, I don't recall any city mentioned that has fallen to Spectres and is controlled by them. The aforementioned places of calamity and violence are good candidates, in which a city was left open for invasion. Hiroshima and Nagasaki come to mind if they ever were part of the Dark Kingdom of Iron in the first place.<br /> <br /> - Skinland Necropoles<br /> Conjecture, but I would assume places with unusually large burial sites attract a large number of Wraiths that could result in independent communities. Paris is a good example.<br /> <br /> - Large cities outside of the Dark Kingdom of Iron<br /> This one should be obvious, but it depends on where you draw the borders.<br /> <br /> Overall it seems to be largely up to the ST how they want their cities in their chronicles.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Atticus Cleanclaw</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1532251-necropolii#post1532251\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Incidentally, it has also occurred to me to wonder how long it took for the living city to which a Necropolis was attached to equal the population of it\u2019s sepulchral counterpart (Given that for much of human history people lived fast and died young).</div> </div> </div> </div>Hard to say, but I would assume at least a four or five generation delay until a somewhat sizable Wraith population accumulates. I generally don't think a Necropolis has anywhere near an equal population to its Skinland counterpart. Becoming a Wraith is an exception, and Wraiths don't stick around forever either.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Custom Sorcery: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1487084-pattern-weaving-ananasi-sorcery-homebrew\" target=\"_blank\">For Ananasi</a> | Mortal:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1509365-hekau-for-sorcerers\" target=\"_blank\"> Hekau</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1550552-ma%C5%A1ma%C5%A1%C5%A1%C5%ABtu-mesopotamian-sorcery\" target=\"_blank\">Ma\u0161ma\u0161\u0161\u016btu</a> | Necromancy:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1514352\" target=\"_blank\"> Dust Path</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535508-dav20-older-lamia-lore?p=1536244#post1536244\" target=\"_blank\">P.o.Fertile Grave Soil</a> | Thauma: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1515316\" target=\"_blank\">Ars Notoria</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27477-voidshaper","name":"voidshaper","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27477_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"698"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-03-09T16:02:44-08:00","id":1532301,"title":"","content":"If you want to investigate wraith demographics this passage from 'Ends of Empire' detailing London might be of interest:<br /> <br /> &quot;Seven million human beings live in Skinlands London. The vast majority don\u2019t become wraiths when they die \u2014 less than one soul in 20 emerges across the Shroud in an environment as relatively calm and peaceful as the Great Britain of the 1990s. Far more people become wraiths upon death in times of great calamity and suffering, and wraiths can last (with care and luck) a very long time. In the modern day, about a million active wraiths inhabit the London Necropolis&quot;<br /> <br /> Do keep in mind that London isn't a typical example. It's ancient, American cities of similar size will not have built up a wraithly population quite this large. Other European metropoli might not quite measure up either, cause London is known as the &quot;First Necropolis&quot;. In the Third Great Maelstrom's wake it was Stygia's first major outpost in the Shadowlands. Then again; mainland cities have seen more war, and other atrocities, in recent centuries.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2198-malkavian87","name":"Malkavian87","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2198_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"325"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-03-10T11:06:53-07:00","id":1532339,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>voidshaper</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1532251-necropolii?p=1532286#post1532286\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Hard to say, but I would assume at least a four or five generation delay until a somewhat sizable Wraith population accumulates. I generally don't think a Necropolis has anywhere near an equal population to its Skinland counterpart. Becoming a Wraith is an exception, and Wraiths don't stick around forever either.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not even sure that a Wraith's &quot;Death Expectancy&quot; out passes the life expectancy of the quick, even in previous eras when life expectancy was abysmal. Considering that between the outcomes for Wraiths you have being torn into a chair or a sword or a wall, being dragged and mind/soul raped by spectres, devoured by Oblivion or yes, maybe even &quot;transcendence&quot; (although that is so small that it won't influence the statistics at all), I'm not sure that Wraiths have such a good chance at lingering for long. Centuries old (or even millennia old) would probably be the exceptions of the exceptions, with the vast majority of wraiths &quot;living fast and dying young&quot; too just as much as the &quot;quick&quot;<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-03-11T04:59:17-07:00","id":1532365,"title":"","content":"Thank You to everyone who has taken the time to answer my queries.<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> I must admit to an unseemly amusement at the semantic confusion frequently involved when trying to discuss the afterlife of Wraiths: they were absolutely dead to begin with, but we are still obliged to note that they \u2018lived fast and died young\u2019 due to the limits of any living language (and even Dead Languages seem easy to confuse when it comes to discussing beings that are technically dead, but still going about some sort of business - or at least discussing them in any detail).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28339-atticus-cleanclaw","name":"Atticus Cleanclaw","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2024"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"638"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-06-20T05:03:25-07:00","id":1537687,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magus Maximus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1532251-necropolii?p=1532339#post1532339\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I'm not even sure that a Wraith's &quot;Death Expectancy&quot; out passes the life expectancy of the quick, even in previous eras when life expectancy was abysmal. Considering that between the outcomes for Wraiths you have being torn into a chair or a sword or a wall, being dragged and mind/soul raped by spectres, devoured by Oblivion or yes, maybe even &quot;transcendence&quot; (although that is so small that it won't influence the statistics at all), I'm not sure that Wraiths have such a good chance at lingering for long. Centuries old (or even millennia old) would probably be the exceptions of the exceptions, with the vast majority of wraiths &quot;living fast and dying young&quot; too just as much as the &quot;quick&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Just yesterday I thought that this was somewhat one of the &quot;weaknesses&quot; of WtO compared ot the other games of the original line.<br /> The other games usually built up societies with secret masters and their wars that spanned decades, centuries, millenia even.<br /> But the setting gave the impression that with the exception of the deathlords and other such characters, having very old or even ancient wraiths was very rare due to all the dangers and risks and whatnot that made it statistically impossible.<br /> Of course this is the impression and it's really difficult to know how the majority of the player base handled the situation of ancient ghosts<br /> <br /> I recall a quick reading of Necropolis Atlanta before ever reading WtO, but I never owned the book. In that book I recall there were a few ghosts that they were at least a century old, but maybe I recall badly.<br /> Yet after owning the rulebook I got the impression the concept was pushed to be very unlikely. (to the detriment of the setting IMHO)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Ever since the Followers of Set rebranded themselves as The Ministry, I can barely keep a straight face around them.&quot; - Ramona","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/15944-ravnos","name":"Ravnos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar15944_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"151"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-06-20T10:54:33-07:00","id":1537715,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ravnos</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/1532251-necropolii?p=1537687#post1537687\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Just yesterday I thought that this was somewhat one of the &quot;weaknesses&quot; of WtO compared ot the other games of the original line.<br /> The other games usually built up societies with secret masters and their wars that spanned decades, centuries, millenia even.<br /> But the setting gave the impression that with the exception of the deathlords and other such characters, having very old or even ancient wraiths was very rare due to all the dangers and risks and whatnot that made it statistically impossible.<br /> Of course this is the impression and it's really difficult to know how the majority of the player base handled the situation of ancient ghosts<br /> <br /> I recall a quick reading of Necropolis Atlanta before ever reading WtO, but I never owned the book. In that book I recall there were a few ghosts that they were at least a century old, but maybe I recall badly.<br /> Yet after owning the rulebook I got the impression the concept was pushed to be very unlikely. (to the detriment of the setting IMHO)</div> </div> </div> </div>My view on it as close to my view on VtM. Most of the ones to perish are the &quot;toddlers&quot;; youngsters are the highest demographic to be subjected to final death.<br /> <br /> After a certain threshold, final death becomes very rare (possibly after a century). That marks an individual that has become well connected, powerful (both in terms of influence over their environment and in their supernatural powers) and has acquired the cunning and wisdom and knowledge to keep going.<br /> <br /> So, after that threshold, the population declines only so slowly.<br /> <br /> That also holds true for Wraiths, but with some more decline over the ages than for vampires, because their world is far more dangerous, even for the ancient ones. Even Charon was not free from the slow pull of Oblivion. But, the older you get, the best chances you'll have to keep getting older. So, for instance, if there's 1M wraiths over 100 to 199 years globally (I'm just throwing an hypothetical number here), than probably there would be 900k with 200 to 299, 850K between 300 and 399 and so forth, something like that","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28035-magus-maximus","name":"Magus Maximus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"569"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-06-20T11:42:11-07:00","id":1537719,"title":"","content":"For the purposes of the game, most ghosts in the Shadowlands are young. There are some old wraiths in the Shadowlands, but they need their Fetters to be there. The need for Fetters limits how many old ghosts can really be in the Necropoli to manage things. Really old ghosts that lost their Fetters have to reside in Stygia. I imagine most STs do not make Stygia an important part of their chronicles, and that most PCs either never visit or visit very little. If you want the PCs to encounter older, powerful Gaunts, an ST needs to utilize Stygia more.<br /> <br /> Stygia probably would be utilized more except there's never been a sourcebook for it. While some descriptions have been provided in several sourcebooks, it still means every ST would have to put in a lot of work to make a &quot;living&quot; city that would inspire STs and PCs and provide lots of hooks. Much like we never got a book on the Heretics, a full blown sourcebook on Stygia was one of the great failures of developing the game line.<br /> <br /> Long term survival as a wraith is really about how strong their Shadow is and whether it can overwhelm the Psyche and turn the wraith into a spectre. That contest is probably determined fairly early - probably within a few decades for most wraiths. Any wraith that survives to a century can probably expect a long time of existence, though probably in Stygia.<br /> <br /> There's always going to a certain amount of decline in all age cohorts over the years through violence, Maelstroms, spectres, finally falling to one's Shadow, and so on, but it is probably small most of the time for those wraiths not actively involved in military operations of the Legions. The big exceptions would be the times of the Great Maelstroms when all hell breaks loose and many wraiths of all ages and anywhere in the netherworld are destroyed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}